WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.100
Node.js dependencies are not one simple list.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:10.600
package.json declares intended dependencies, scripts, metadata, and version ranges.

3
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:19.100
Lockfiles such as package-lock.json or npm-shrinkwrap.json describe resolved versions and dependency relationships.

4
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.560
Workspaces add multiple package manifests.

5
00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:25.620
node_modules represents an installed tree.

6
00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:34.120
Build outputs, bundled files, Docker images, and deployed artifacts may contain a different view of the software than the source repository alone.

7
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:42.720
Dependencies may be direct, transitive, development-only, production runtime, optional, peer, bundled, or workspace-local.

8
00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:52.260
Native modules, generated code, vendored files, container base images, operating system packages, and runtime images may also matter depending on scope.

9
00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:55.080
None of these categories should be ignored by default.

10
00:00:55.180 --> 00:01:08.860
If dev dependencies, optional dependencies, peer dependencies, workspace packages, bundled code, native modules, or container packages are excluded, that should be a documented scope decision tied to the SBOM's purpose.

11
00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:13.780
Before judging whether an SBOM is complete, define the artifact it represents.

12
00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:22.440
Is the SBOM for a source project, npm package, build artifact, container image, deployed service, or customer release.

13
00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:27.740
A source dependency SBOM may answer one question, while a container SBOM may answer another.

14
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.720
The right inventory depends on the operational question.

15
00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:36.580
Good SBOM work begins by naming the artifact and then matching the dependency evidence to that artifact.
